s is the killer condition. The test that accounts for this is the Moisture Vapor Transmission Rate (MVTR) test, often conducted with the fabric in a "wetted-out" state. Here's a common version (the inverted cup method): A cup is filled with water, and the fabric is sealed over the top. The whole assembly is weighed, then placed face-down in a controlled, windy environment. Water vapor moves through the fabric and escapes. After a period of time, the cup is re-weighed. The loss of water mass is measured, giving a grams-of-water-vapor-per-square-meter-per-24-hours (g/m²/24hr) rating. Crucially, this test can be run with the fabric *dry* and with the outer face *in constant contact with liquid water*. This simulates exactly what you described: a soaked jacket. The results show that breathability plummets dramatically when the outer face is wet, confirming your experience. However, it also shows that it doesn't drop to zero. Vapor continues to escape, just at a significantly reduced rate. The claim isn't "breathability is unaffected by rain"; it's "it continues to breathe at a reduced rate while remaining absolutely impervious to liquid water ingress, even under pressure." ### So, What This Proves About Your Experience When you're in heavy rain: 1. The membrane is passing the hydrostatic head test in real-time. The cold, wind-driven rain is exerting pressure far below the membrane's failure point. **No liquid rain is getting in.** The waterproofing is proven. 2. The membrane is simultaneously passing the inverted-cup MVTR test. Sweat vapor is still being driven out, but the rate has collapsed because the exit is now clogged with a cold, wet outer fabric. The breathability is working, just at a small fraction of its dry-weather capacity. The dampness you feel isn't a failure of the waterproofing. It's not a failure of the membrane's ability to let vapor through at all. It's your body generating sweat vapor at a rate of, say, 5,000 g/m²/24hr, while the jacket's rain-soaked fabric is only able to exhaust 1,000 g/m²/24hr. The excess backs up and condenses. The system is overwhelmed, but it's still functioning. You are a human engine that can easily outstrip the technology's peak performance. That's the real story your experience is telling. It's not that the membrane doesn't work; it's that its working capacity is finite, and you can feel that limit as clamminess. The kitchen experiment was an oversimplification, but the lab tests are the brutal, quantified reality that proves the material does exactly what I've described, and your damp jacket is the proof of its limitations, not its falsehood.
